  ('6ef1fbbb-aa20-510c-aec1-eb137471de58', v_en_id, $J$substrand$J$, $J$Different Jobs, One Colony$J$, $J$Key concepts: Division of Labor (Age Polyethism), Colony as Superorganism$J$, $J$GB-2$J$, $J$Biology$J$, $J${"source_id":"OE-STRAND-bee-k12-hive-superorganism-gb2","performanceIndicators":["Compares the different jobs bees take on within a colony and explains why no single bee does all of them.","Applies the colony-as-superorganism idea to a new example of group behavior in nature."],"concepts":[{"id":"OE-CONCEPT-bee-k12-division-of-labor","emphasis":"primary"},{"id":"OE-CONCEPT-bee-k12-superorganism","emphasis":"reinforcing"}],"required":true,"typicalGradeBand":"~8–10 (Kl. 3–4)","gradeBandId":"GB-2","_source":{"repo":"bee-k12","path":"lpm/strands/strand-hive-superorganism.yaml"}}$J$::jsonb, $J$draft$J$),
  ('1548cf62-182f-582e-98b6-8755bea87da0', v_en_id, $J$substrand$J$, $J$Deciding Without a Boss$J$, $J$Key concepts: Division of Labor (Age Polyethism)$J$, $J$GB-3$J$, $J$Biology$J$, $J${"source_id":"OE-STRAND-bee-k12-hive-superorganism-gb3","performanceIndicators":["Models, using a simple payoff matrix, how individual choices can add up to a group decision without central control.","Compares division of labor in a bee colony to division of labor in a human organization the student is part of."],"concepts":[{"id":"OE-CONCEPT-oe-interdisciplinary-collective-intelligence","emphasis":"primary"},{"id":"OE-CONCEPT-bee-k12-division-of-labor","emphasis":"reinforcing"}],"required":true,"typicalGradeBand":"~10–12 (Kl. 5–6)","gradeBandId":"GB-3","_source":{"repo":"bee-k12","path":"lpm/strands/strand-hive-superorganism.yaml"}}$J$::jsonb, $J$draft$J$),
  ('4214ce0e-b801-5c5d-938e-151cc18a5df5', v_en_id, $J$substrand$J$, $J$How the Message Gets Around$J$, $J$Key concepts: Waggle Dance Communication, Swarm Nest-Site Decision-Making, Colony as Superorganism$J$, $J$GB-4$J$, $J$Biology$J$, $J${"source_id":"OE-STRAND-bee-k12-hive-superorganism-gb4","performanceIndicators":["Explains how the waggle dance's angle and duration encode the direction and distance of a resource.","Models, with a simple diagram, how competing scout dances and a quorum threshold let a swarm choose a single nest site without central control.","Compares the bee colony's collective decision process to one human group decision process (e.g. a class vote, a jury)."],"concepts":[{"id":"OE-CONCEPT-bee-k12-waggle-dance-communication","emphasis":"primary"},{"id":"OE-CONCEPT-bee-k12-swarm-decision-making","emphasis":"primary"},{"id":"OE-CONCEPT-bee-k12-superorganism","emphasis":"reinforcing"}],"required":false,"typicalGradeBand":"~12–14 (Kl. 7–8)","gradeBandId":"GB-4","_source":{"repo":"bee-k12","path":"lpm/strands/strand-hive-superorganism.yaml"}}$J$::jsonb, $J$draft$J$),
  ('747ba708-4230-5400-9f95-172e0cb346e0', v_en_id, $J$substrand$J$, $J$Division of Labor as an Economic Idea$J$, $J$Key concepts: Division of Labor (Age Polyethism)$J$, $J$GB-5$J$, $J$Biology$J$, $J${"source_id":"OE-STRAND-bee-k12-hive-superorganism-gb5","performanceIndicators":["Compares division of labor in a bee colony to specialization in a human market economy, and identifies one cost and one benefit of specialization in each case."],"concepts":[{"id":"OE-CONCEPT-bee-k12-division-of-labor","emphasis":"reinforcing"}],"required":false,"typicalGradeBand":"~14–16 (Kl. 9–10)","gradeBandId":"GB-5","_source":{"repo":"bee-k12","path":"lpm/strands/strand-hive-superorganism.yaml"}}$J$::jsonb, $J$draft$J$),
  ('5ea5aa32-7af9-58c7-8099-31419e00a00f', v_en_id, $J$substrand$J$, $J$The Colony as a Unit of Selection$J$, $J$Key concepts: Major Transitions in Individuality, 90 Percent Chimp, 10 Percent Bee, Colony as Superorganism$J$, $J$GB-6$J$, $J$Biology$J$, $J${"source_id":"OE-STRAND-bee-k12-hive-superorganism-gb6","performanceIndicators":["Explains, using the honeybee colony as a case, how formerly independent reproducing units can come to function as a single higher-level unit of selection.","Connects the colony-as-superorganism idea introduced in early grades to the formal evolutionary-biology concept of a major transition in individuality.","Evaluates one argument for and one argument against treating the colony, rather than the individual bee, as the primary unit that natural selection acts on.","Builds an analogy map comparing at least three features (e.g. cooperation, conflict suppression, boundary/nestmate recognition, social learning) across bees, chimpanzees, and humans, explicitly naming for each feature both what maps and what doesn't.","Explains Haidt's claim that humans are \"90 percent chimp and 10 percent bee,\" restates it as an explicit metaphor rather than a literal proportion (citing Haidt's own caution that bee and human psychology have nothing in common), and identifies which of Tinbergen's/SpeciesBase's explanatory questions (mechanism, function, phylogeny) the metaphor addresses and which it leaves open.","Sorts a set of claims about the analogy into 'integrated reasoning' versus named misconceptions (false uniqueness bias -- assuming only humans have the capacity at all; false equivalence -- treating the bee and human mechanisms as literally the same kind of process), following the same misconception-taxonomy pattern used elsewhere in the OpenEvo ecosystem for cross-species cognition assessment."],"concepts":[{"id":"OE-CONCEPT-bee-k12-major-transitions-individuality","emphasis":"primary"},{"id":"OE-CONCEPT-bee-k12-90-percent-chimp-10-percent-bee","emphasis":"primary"},{"id":"OE-CONCEPT-bee-k12-superorganism","emphasis":"reinforcing"},{"id":"OE-CONCEPT-oe-interdisciplinary-selection","emphasis":"reinforcing"}],"required":true,"typicalGradeBand":"~16–19 (Kl. 11–13)","gradeBandId":"GB-6","_source":{"repo":"bee-k12","path":"lpm/strands/strand-hive-superorganism.yaml"}}$J$::jsonb, $J$draft$J$),
  ('3f9a7a63-e23a-578e-a289-1aba4f85e249', v_en_id, $J$strand$J$, $J$Bees & Ecological Interdependence$J$, $J$How bees connect to the wider food web through pollination — from the simple, sensory observation that bees visit flowers, through the biological mechanism of pollination and the idea of an ecosystem service, to the economics and food-security stakes of a food system that depends on animal pollination, largely via managed rather than wild bees.
$J$, $J$$J$, $J$Biology$J$, $J${"source_id":"OE-STRAND-bee-k12-ecological-interdependence","description":"How bees connect to the wider food web through pollination — from the simple, sensory observation that bees visit flowers, through the biological mechanism of pollination and the idea of an ecosystem service, to the economics and food-security stakes of a food system that depends on animal pollination, largely via managed rather than wild bees.\n","associatedDomains":["Biology","Geography"],"typicalGradeBand":"GB-0–GB-6 (spans all bands via subStrands; primary weight at GB-5–GB-6)","relations":{"parallel":["OE-STRAND-bee-k12-economics-managed-resource"]},"homeAnchors":[{"id":"food-web-dependencies","label":"Food-Web Dependencies","description":"How organisms and food systems depend on services (like pollination) provided by other species."},{"id":"biodiversity-loss","label":"Biodiversity Loss","description":"Causes and consequences of declining species diversity, including knock-on effects for managed systems."},{"id":"agricultural-systems","label":"Agricultural Systems","description":"How farming systems are structured around, and made vulnerable by, dependence on specific ecological services."},{"id":"responsibility-and-stewardship","label":"Responsibility & Stewardship","description":"Who bears responsibility when a shared ecological resource is degraded or depleted."}],"thinkingTool":{"id":"cause-maps","label":"Cause Maps","description":"Diagrams that trace a single observed effect back to multiple, distinct kinds of contributing causes.","status":"confirmed"},"essentialQuestion":{"broad":"How can insights about behavior be applied to sustainability?","worked":"Much of our food crop supply benefits at least partly from animal pollination, largely via managed rather than wild bees. What happens to agriculture as managed pollinators decline — and who is responsible?"},"epistemicStatus":"designed-thought-experiment","_source":{"repo":"bee-k12","path":"lpm/strands/strand-ecological-interdependence.yaml"}}$J$::jsonb, $J$draft$J$),
