  ('ab486abc-db1e-5cbb-8fdd-104c25e28129', v_en_id, $J$substrand$J$, $J$A Food System That Depends on Pollination$J$, $J$Key concepts: Pollination Dependency, Managed vs. Wild Pollinators, Ecosystem Service$J$, $J$GB-5$J$, $J$Biology$J$, $J${"source_id":"OE-STRAND-bee-k12-ecological-interdependence-gb5","performanceIndicators":["Explains, with a specific crop example (e.g. almonds), why a food plant depends on animal pollination and estimates what is at stake if that pollination fails.","Compares the role of managed honeybee colonies to wild pollinators in maintaining food-crop yields, citing at least one real figure (e.g. IPBES's estimate that roughly three-quarters of food crops benefit at least partially from animal pollination)."],"concepts":[{"id":"OE-CONCEPT-bee-k12-pollination-dependency","emphasis":"primary"},{"id":"OE-CONCEPT-bee-k12-managed-vs-wild-pollinators","emphasis":"primary"},{"id":"OE-CONCEPT-bee-k12-ecosystem-service","emphasis":"reinforcing"}],"required":true,"typicalGradeBand":"~14–16 (Kl. 9–10)","gradeBandId":"GB-5","_source":{"repo":"bee-k12","path":"lpm/strands/strand-ecological-interdependence.yaml"}}$J$::jsonb, $J$draft$J$),
  ('a5444fad-d15f-5a51-b373-860bc0cd2e13', v_en_id, $J$substrand$J$, $J$Pollinator Decline and Food Security$J$, $J$Key concepts: Colony and Pollinator Decline, Pollination Dependency, Managed vs. Wild Pollinators$J$, $J$GB-6$J$, $J$Biology$J$, $J${"source_id":"OE-STRAND-bee-k12-ecological-interdependence-gb6","performanceIndicators":["Identifies at least three distinct contributing causes of colony and pollinator decline (e.g. habitat loss, pesticide exposure, Varroa destructor, climate change) and explains that no single cause is sufficient on its own.","Evaluates a proposed policy or management response to pollinator decline in terms of who bears its cost and who benefits from it."],"concepts":[{"id":"OE-CONCEPT-bee-k12-colony-and-pollinator-decline","emphasis":"primary"},{"id":"OE-CONCEPT-bee-k12-pollination-dependency","emphasis":"reinforcing"},{"id":"OE-CONCEPT-bee-k12-managed-vs-wild-pollinators","emphasis":"reinforcing"}],"required":true,"typicalGradeBand":"~16–19 (Kl. 11–13)","gradeBandId":"GB-6","_source":{"repo":"bee-k12","path":"lpm/strands/strand-ecological-interdependence.yaml"}}$J$::jsonb, $J$draft$J$),
  ('853d5da8-d33f-5420-84c6-8535d484c233', v_en_id, $J$strand$J$, $J$The Economics & Law of a Managed Resource$J$, $J$How a biological resource — pollination, honey, wax — becomes valuable enough to require its own markets, guilds, courts, and risk management. Concentrated at the top of the progression (GB-5/GB-6), where economics and history first appear as dedicated subjects, rather than spread thin across bands the way Strands A and B are.
$J$, $J$$J$, $J$Economics/Civics$J$, $J${"source_id":"OE-STRAND-bee-k12-economics-managed-resource","description":"How a biological resource — pollination, honey, wax — becomes valuable enough to require its own markets, guilds, courts, and risk management. Concentrated at the top of the progression (GB-5/GB-6), where economics and history first appear as dedicated subjects, rather than spread thin across bands the way Strands A and B are.\n","associatedDomains":["Economics/Civics","History"],"typicalGradeBand":"GB-0–GB-6 (spans all bands via subStrands; carries real weight only at GB-5–GB-6)","relations":{"parallel":["OE-STRAND-bee-k12-ecological-interdependence"]},"homeAnchors":[{"id":"markets-for-ecosystem-services","label":"Markets for Ecosystem Services","description":"How a naturally occurring service (like pollination) becomes a priced, tradeable service."},{"id":"commons-governance-historical","label":"Historical Commons Governance","description":"Historical institutions (guilds, dedicated courts) that societies built to govern a valuable shared resource."},{"id":"substitute-goods-and-obsolescence","label":"Substitute Goods & Obsolescence","description":"What happens to a trade, product, or regional economy when a substitute displaces the original good."},{"id":"risk-and-insurance","label":"Risk & Insurance","description":"How managed enterprises (like a school apiary) price and hedge against loss and uncertainty."}],"thinkingTool":{"id":"cause-maps","label":"Cause Maps","description":"Diagrams that trace a single observed effect back to multiple, distinct kinds of contributing causes.","status":"confirmed"},"essentialQuestion":{"broad":"What can communities that manage a shared resource teach us?","worked":"Medieval Zeidler (wild-honey gatherers) had their own guild, their own court, and special rights — because honey and wax were valuable enough to need their own rules. What happens to a trade when a substitute product makes the original obsolete?"},"epistemicStatus":"designed-thought-experiment","_source":{"repo":"bee-k12","path":"lpm/strands/strand-economics-managed-resource.yaml"}}$J$::jsonb, $J$draft$J$),
