  ('a99ce459-812f-5477-a695-a822647a89f4', v_en_id, $J$substrand$J$, $J$A Resource Worth Its Own Rules$J$, $J$Key concepts: Pollination Markets, Zeidler Guild Law$J$, $J$GB-5$J$, $J$Economics/Civics$J$, $J${"source_id":"OE-STRAND-bee-k12-economics-managed-resource-gb5","performanceIndicators":["Explains how pollination became a paid service (e.g. trucked colonies for the California almond harvest) and calculates a simple supply-and-demand scenario for a pollination market.","Describes why the medieval Zeidler trade developed its own guild and court (e.g. the Zeidelgericht in Feucht, active from the late 13th to late 18th century), and identifies the conditions that make a resource valuable enough to require special governance.","Names one other historical or modern institution (guild, cooperative, regulatory body) that governs access to a valuable shared resource, and compares it to Zeidler guild law."],"concepts":[{"id":"OE-CONCEPT-bee-k12-pollination-markets","emphasis":"primary"},{"id":"OE-CONCEPT-bee-k12-zeidler-guild-law","emphasis":"primary"},{"id":"OE-CONCEPT-oe-interdisciplinary-institutions","emphasis":"reinforcing"}],"required":true,"typicalGradeBand":"~14–16 (Kl. 9–10)","gradeBandId":"GB-5","_source":{"repo":"bee-k12","path":"lpm/strands/strand-economics-managed-resource.yaml"}}$J$::jsonb, $J$draft$J$),
  ('9049ef02-57b7-5464-8c5b-160afab0aabb', v_en_id, $J$substrand$J$, $J$When a Substitute Displaces the Original$J$, $J$Key concepts: Substitute-Good Displacement, Apiary Risk & Insurance, Pollination Markets$J$, $J$GB-6$J$, $J$Economics/Civics$J$, $J${"source_id":"OE-STRAND-bee-k12-economics-managed-resource-gb6","performanceIndicators":["Analyzes, using the decline of Zeidlerei after cane sugar (and later sugar beet) became available, what happens to a regional economy when a substitute product displaces the original good, and generalizes the pattern to one other historical or modern example.","Calculates the economics of a small managed apiary (e.g. the school apiary's honey sales), including at least two distinct risk factors (e.g. colony loss, weather, disease) and how a beekeeper might hedge against each."],"concepts":[{"id":"OE-CONCEPT-bee-k12-substitute-good-displacement","emphasis":"primary"},{"id":"OE-CONCEPT-bee-k12-apiary-risk-and-insurance","emphasis":"primary"},{"id":"OE-CONCEPT-bee-k12-pollination-markets","emphasis":"reinforcing"}],"required":true,"typicalGradeBand":"~16–19 (Kl. 11–13)","gradeBandId":"GB-6","_source":{"repo":"bee-k12","path":"lpm/strands/strand-economics-managed-resource.yaml"}}$J$::jsonb, $J$draft$J$),
  ('045012f3-8f49-5b52-b01e-93b83cfe9450', v_en_id, $J$strand$J$, $J$Bee Cognition & Swarm Intelligence$J$, $J$What a brain the size of a grass seed can and can't do — from noticing that a bee seems to remember a good flower, through the real mechanism of associative learning (proboscis-extension-reflex conditioning, the mushroom body), to computational models (the Artificial Bee Colony algorithm) that translate collective foraging behavior into algorithms solving real optimization problems.
$J$, $J$$J$, $J$Biology$J$, $J${"source_id":"OE-STRAND-bee-k12-cognition-swarm-intelligence","description":"What a brain the size of a grass seed can and can't do — from noticing that a bee seems to remember a good flower, through the real mechanism of associative learning (proboscis-extension-reflex conditioning, the mushroom body), to computational models (the Artificial Bee Colony algorithm) that translate collective foraging behavior into algorithms solving real optimization problems.\n","associatedDomains":["Biology","Mathematics/Computer Science","Physics"],"typicalGradeBand":"GB-0–GB-6 (spans all bands via subStrands; primary weight at GB-4–GB-6)","relations":{"parallel":["OE-STRAND-bee-k12-hive-superorganism","OE-STRAND-bee-k12-evolution-deep-time"]},"homeAnchors":[{"id":"associative-learning","label":"Associative Learning","description":"How an organism learns to link a neutral stimulus with a meaningful outcome through repeated pairing."},{"id":"small-brain-cognition","label":"Small-Brain Cognition","description":"What a very small nervous system can and can't accomplish, and what that reveals about cognition generally."},{"id":"computational-models-of-behavior","label":"Computational Models of Behavior","description":"Translating an observed biological behavior into an algorithm that solves a real computational problem."},{"id":"heuristics-vs-planning","label":"Heuristics vs. Planning","description":"When a simple, repeated rule outperforms a single deliberate plan."},{"id":"perceptual-worlds-umwelt","label":"Perceptual Worlds (Umwelt)","description":"How different a species' sensory Umwelt can be from a human's — using a documented sensory capacity (e.g. UV color vision) to make the difference concrete rather than abstract."}],"thinkingTool":{"id":"computational-agent-based-models","label":"Computational / Agent-Based Models","description":"Simplified rule-based simulations (such as the Artificial Bee Colony algorithm) that model many simple agents to re