  ('1ceb8912-d669-5023-bc6e-e9f86a2ef317', v_en_id, $J$substrand$J$, $J$Bees That Enforce the Rules$J$, $J$Key concepts: Worker Policing, Nestmate Recognition$J$, $J$GB-4$J$, $J$Biology$J$, $J${"source_id":"OE-STRAND-bee-k12-evolution-deep-time-gb4","performanceIndicators":["Describes worker policing (workers destroying other workers' eggs) and queen pheromone as two distinct mechanisms that keep reproduction skewed toward the queen despite every worker having functional ovaries.","Distinguishes this within-colony conflict-suppression from nestmate recognition, the chemical-cue mechanism guard bees use to reject non-nestmates at the hive entrance."],"concepts":[{"id":"OE-CONCEPT-bee-k12-worker-policing","emphasis":"primary"},{"id":"OE-CONCEPT-bee-k12-nestmate-recognition","emphasis":"primary"}],"required":true,"typicalGradeBand":"~12–14 (Kl. 7–8)","gradeBandId":"GB-4","_source":{"repo":"bee-k12","path":"lpm/strands/strand-evolution-deep-time.yaml"}}$J$::jsonb, $J$draft$J$),
  ('79bdc8e4-b567-5247-9aa4-849e1c697d86', v_en_id, $J$substrand$J$, $J$Why Give Up Your Own Reproduction?$J$, $J$Key concepts: Kin Selection and Altruism$J$, $J$GB-5$J$, $J$Biology$J$, $J${"source_id":"OE-STRAND-bee-k12-evolution-deep-time-gb5","performanceIndicators":["Uses a basic relatedness calculation to explain why worker bees forgo their own reproduction to raise the queen's offspring.","Distinguishes kin selection from ordinary individual-level natural selection, and states what fitness means at the level of a gene passed on via relatives."],"concepts":[{"id":"OE-CONCEPT-bee-k12-kin-selection-and-altruism","emphasis":"primary"}],"required":true,"typicalGradeBand":"~14–16 (Kl. 9–10)","gradeBandId":"GB-5","_source":{"repo":"bee-k12","path":"lpm/strands/strand-evolution-deep-time.yaml"}}$J$::jsonb, $J$draft$J$),
  ('c063550e-7c34-5901-b496-afef6f7fda0b', v_en_id, $J$substrand$J$, $J$Four Questions About One Behavior$J$, $J$Key concepts: Evolutionary Origins of Cooperation, Kin Selection and Altruism, Major Transitions in Individuality, Worker Policing$J$, $J$GB-6$J$, $J$Biology$J$, $J${"source_id":"OE-STRAND-bee-k12-evolution-deep-time-gb6","performanceIndicators":["Sorts competing explanations for a cooperative behavior into Tinbergen's four categories (mechanism, development, function, evolution) and identifies which category is most often skipped.","Compares kin-selected altruism in bee colonies with the development of fairness behavior in human children, using at least one concrete example of each.","Connects kin selection to Strand A's major-transitions-in-individuality idea: explains why suppressing reproductive conflict among relatives is a precondition for a colony to function as a single higher-level unit of selection.","Uses worker policing (GB-4) as a concrete mechanistic example of the reproductive-conflict suppression this major-transitions connection depends on, rather than leaving the claim at the level of an unexplained assertion."],"concepts":[{"id":"OE-CONCEPT-bee-k12-evolutionary-origins-of-cooperation","emphasis":"primary"},{"id":"OE-CONCEPT-bee-k12-kin-selection-and-altruism","emphasis":"reinforcing"},{"id":"OE-CONCEPT-bee-k12-major-transitions-individuality","emphasis":"reinforcing"},{"id":"OE-CONCEPT-bee-k12-worker-policing","emphasis":"reinforcing"}],"required":true,"typicalGradeBand":"~16–19 (Kl. 11–13)","gradeBandId":"GB-6","_source":{"repo":"bee-k12","path":"lpm/strands/strand-evolution-deep-time.yaml"}}$J$::jsonb, $J$draft$J$),
  ('f3e8bf1c-8f78-501e-829f-66da24c5787d', v_en_id, $J$strand$J$, $J$Apiculture as Craft & Management Philosophy$J$, $J$Conventional vs. nature-appropriate (wesensgemäß) beekeeping practice, disease management as a communal task, and — because the pilot site has a real school apiary — a practical thread that runs the whole length of the progression: early-grade observation and harvest festivals, supervised colony inspections in the middle grades, real apiary data as teaching material, and a capstone apiary-based inquiry at the top.
$J$, $J$$J$, $J$Biology$J$, $J${"source_id":"OE-STRAND-bee-k12-apiculture-craft-philosophy","description":"Conventional vs. nature-appropriate (wesensgemäß) beekeeping practice, disease management as a communal task, and — because the pilot site has a real school apiary — a practical thread that runs the whole length of the progression: early-grade observation and harvest festivals, supervised colony inspections in the middle grades, real apiary data as teaching material, and a capstone apiary-based inquiry at the top.\n","associatedDomains":["Biology","History","Physics","Chemistry","Crafts/Design/Tech","Ethics/Religion"],"typicalGradeBand":"GB-0–GB-6 (spans all bands via subStrands; primary weight at GB-3, GB-4)","relations":{"foundationalTo":[],"parallel":["OE-STRAND-bee-k12-hive-superorganism"]},"homeAnchors":[{"id":"management-philosophies","label":"Management Philosophies","description":"How differing convictions about caring for a living system lead to genuinely different management decisions."},{"id":"craft-traditions","label":"Craft Traditions","description":"Hands-on trade knowledge and tool-building passed down and practiced as a craft."},{"id":"integrated-pest-management","label":"Integrated Pest Management","description":"Deciding when and how to intervene against a pest based on measured thresholds rather than a fixed schedule."},{"id":"cross-cultural-practice-comparison","label":"Cross-Cultural Practice Comparison","description":"Comparing differing human practices around the same living system to surface the values behind each."}],"thinkingTool":{"id":"cause-maps","label":"Cause Maps","description":"Diagrams that trace a single observed effect back to multiple, distinct kinds of contributing causes.","status":"confirmed"},"essentialQuestion":{"broad":"What can we learn from the diversity of human cultures and practices?","worked":"Two beekeepers can look at the same colony and make opposite decisions. What does it mean to tend a living system accordi